1
00:00:01,070 --> 00:00:03,190
Hello everyone and welcome to EnglishPod.

2
00:00:03,430 --> 00:00:03,830
My name is Marco.

3
00:00:04,330 --> 00:00:09,590
My name is Catherine and today we have an elementary level lesson in which we're going to talk about small talk.

4
00:00:10,230 --> 00:00:10,750
That's right.

5
00:00:10,750 --> 00:00:20,090
Today we're going to talk to an old friend and so we're going to give you some of the words and phrases you need to talk about your past or talk about how you have been.

6
00:00:20,550 --> 00:00:24,210
So let's listen to today's dialogue and we'll be back in a moment to discuss it.

7
00:00:29,180 --> 00:00:31,620
I hate coming to high school reunions.

8
00:00:31,740 --> 00:00:32,900
It'll be great, honey.

9
00:00:33,600 --> 00:00:37,900
We will get to see your old classmates and catch up to see how they've been doing.

10
00:00:39,220 --> 00:00:40,000
Yeah, I guess so.

11
00:00:40,500 --> 00:00:42,460
Oh look, there's Robert Matthews.

12
00:00:43,220 --> 00:00:43,380
Rob.

13
00:00:43,980 --> 00:00:44,640
Hey, Bill.

14
00:00:45,080 --> 00:00:46,080
Wow.

15
00:00:46,080 --> 00:00:46,640
Great to see you.

16
00:00:47,080 --> 00:00:48,080
Likewise.

17
00:00:48,080 --> 00:00:48,420
It's been a long time.

18
00:00:49,080 --> 00:00:49,720
This is my wife, Dorothy.

19
00:00:50,680 --> 00:00:51,500
Pleasure to meet you.

20
00:00:52,180 --> 00:00:53,100
So Bill, how have you been?

21
00:00:54,000 --> 00:00:55,160
Can't complain.

22
00:00:55,160 --> 00:00:58,120
We have two children who are in college and my business is going well.

23
00:00:58,960 --> 00:00:59,300
How about you?

24
00:00:59,960 --> 00:01:00,880
Ah, you know me.

25
00:01:01,400 --> 00:01:02,440
I'm a dedicated bachelor.

26
00:01:03,280 --> 00:01:06,140
I never married, although I do have a beautiful daughter with Mary.

27
00:01:06,560 --> 00:01:07,140
You remember her.

28
00:01:07,580 --> 00:01:08,800
We were high school sweethearts.

29
00:01:09,400 --> 00:01:12,580
It didn't really work out between us, but I really can't complain either.

30
00:01:13,180 --> 00:01:13,740
That's good.

31
00:01:14,280 --> 00:01:14,840
Have you seen Frank?

32
00:01:15,500 --> 00:01:16,560
I was hoping he would come tonight.

33
00:01:17,200 --> 00:01:17,820
You didn't hear?

34
00:01:18,620 --> 00:01:20,260
Frank passed away last year.

35
00:01:20,950 --> 00:01:21,340
Are you serious?

36
00:01:21,960 --> 00:01:23,500
Nah, I'm just janking your chain.

37
00:01:23,940 --> 00:01:24,480
He'll be here soon.

38
00:01:24,650 --> 00:01:25,500
I saw him last week.

39
00:01:25,960 --> 00:01:30,520
I was hoping he would show up.

40
00:01:30,520 --> 00:01:30,950
Alright we're back.

41
00:01:31,520 --> 00:01:36,520
So now let's get started with our language takeaway.

42
00:01:36,520 --> 00:01:36,960
Language takeaway.

43
00:01:39,240 --> 00:01:45,030
The first phrase we have here in language takeaway is a very important one because it is the setting for today's dialogue.

44
00:01:45,360 --> 00:01:47,960
We are at a high school reunion.

45
00:01:47,960 --> 00:01:48,090
That's right.

46
00:01:48,960 --> 00:01:58,810
So everyone knows what high school is, but a high school reunion is when you go back to your high school for a party maybe five years or ten years after you graduate.

47
00:01:59,280 --> 00:02:02,540
So then you meet all your old friends from high school.

48
00:02:02,800 --> 00:02:08,480
You talk about how you have been and just meet up with everyone that maybe you haven't seen in a very long time.

49
00:02:08,640 --> 00:02:09,140
Exactly.

50
00:02:09,880 --> 00:02:14,580
So a reunion usually happens every five years, ten years, or twenty years.

51
00:02:15,200 --> 00:02:23,120
It can happen at different times, but this is important because you can have many different kinds of reunions like a college reunion or a high school reunion.

52
00:02:23,640 --> 00:02:27,060
I've even heard of some people who had an elementary school reunion.

53
00:02:27,280 --> 00:02:27,440
Really?

54
00:02:28,480 --> 00:02:29,480
Wow.

55
00:02:29,480 --> 00:02:32,360
Imagine seeing your classmates when you were maybe ten years old.

56
00:02:32,360 --> 00:02:33,360
I couldn't imagine.

57
00:02:33,360 --> 00:02:42,960
Alright so we're at a high school reunion and we are there because we're going to meet our old classmates and to catch up and see how they have been.

58
00:02:42,960 --> 00:02:45,360
Okay so this is exactly what you do at a reunion.

59
00:02:45,640 --> 00:02:55,780
You catch up with people that you maybe lost touch with, to catch up means to find out about what people have been doing since the last time you talked.

60
00:02:55,810 --> 00:02:59,630
So maybe Marco and I were classmates in high school and we haven't talked in ten years.

61
00:03:00,350 --> 00:03:01,910
I say, Marco, I want to catch up.

62
00:03:02,010 --> 00:03:07,140
I want to learn about what you do, who you married, where you work, things like that.

63
00:03:07,340 --> 00:03:12,780
Right, so when two people get together to catch up, maybe it doesn't even have to be a very long time.

64
00:03:12,780 --> 00:03:23,020
Maybe you haven't seen your friend for a week and so you want to get together, have a cup of coffee and catch up and see how the relationship has been going or how it works so you want to catch up.

65
00:03:23,480 --> 00:03:27,940
So you can say this in a very simple way, let's catch up or it's been nice to catch up.

66
00:03:28,240 --> 00:03:28,820
That's right.

67
00:03:29,720 --> 00:03:31,840
Alright, moving on to our next word.

68
00:03:31,840 --> 00:03:39,630
This guy Bill meets up with Robert Matthews and they're saying, hey, wow, great to see you and he answers by saying, likewise.

69
00:03:41,080 --> 00:03:42,750
So it's good to see you, likewise.

70
00:03:43,800 --> 00:03:46,090
That means it's good to see you too.

71
00:03:46,770 --> 00:03:49,670
So you don't have to repeat everything that person just said.

72
00:03:49,790 --> 00:03:55,350
For example, if I just meet someone new and I say it's a pleasure to meet you, you can say?

73
00:03:55,770 --> 00:03:56,770
I could say likewise.

74
00:03:57,930 --> 00:04:00,780
Because I don't want to say it's a pleasure to meet you too.

75
00:04:01,200 --> 00:04:01,900
That's so many words.

76
00:04:02,160 --> 00:04:05,560
So likewise is a very short way to say all of those things.

77
00:04:06,240 --> 00:04:06,420
That's right.

78
00:04:07,320 --> 00:04:12,640
So we were finding out about Robert Matthews and he says that, well, he is a dedicated bachelor.

79
00:04:13,860 --> 00:04:17,110
So a dedicated bachelor, what is a bachelor, Marco?

80
00:04:17,360 --> 00:04:21,350
So a bachelor is a person who is not married, a person who is single.

81
00:04:21,670 --> 00:04:22,110
A man.

82
00:04:22,550 --> 00:04:24,390
A woman cannot be a bachelor.

83
00:04:24,650 --> 00:04:34,070
So a bachelor is specifically a man and he's not married and we often have a stereotype, that means we have an idea of what a bachelor lives like.

84
00:04:34,720 --> 00:04:42,370
He has only beer in his refrigerator and he has many relationships and no wife.

85
00:04:43,120 --> 00:04:43,350
That's right.

86
00:04:44,120 --> 00:04:51,500
So a dedicated bachelor is a person who is not married and doesn't really seem to want to get married or any of this.

87
00:04:51,600 --> 00:04:53,800
He wants to stay single.

88
00:04:53,800 --> 00:04:54,800
Exactly.

89
00:04:54,800 --> 00:04:55,800
Okay.

90
00:04:55,800 --> 00:05:03,920
And well, to finish off, this guy Robert lied a little bit to Bill saying that Frank had passed away.

91
00:05:03,920 --> 00:05:08,930
That's really mean, but this phrase to pass away is very important and something we should talk about.

92
00:05:09,480 --> 00:05:11,270
So what does it mean to pass away?

93
00:05:12,040 --> 00:05:14,600
It's another way of saying he died.

94
00:05:15,200 --> 00:05:16,200
So it's nicer.

95
00:05:16,200 --> 00:05:18,360
Yeah, it's a lot more subtle and nicer.

96
00:05:18,640 --> 00:05:23,040
So instead of saying Frank died, you say Frank passed away.

97
00:05:23,520 --> 00:05:26,860
So you could say, you know, last year my grandmother passed away.

98
00:05:27,040 --> 00:05:32,160
That's a way of saying that my grandmother died last year, but again, it's a lot nicer way to say that.

99
00:05:32,160 --> 00:05:37,120
Yeah, because the word died can be a little bit too direct and too harsh.

100
00:05:37,120 --> 00:05:41,100
So maybe saying my grandmother passed away last year, it's a nicer way of saying it.

101
00:05:41,760 --> 00:05:42,760
Okay.

102
00:05:42,760 --> 00:05:44,160
So that's all the language takeaway you have.

103
00:05:44,680 --> 00:05:46,500
Let's take a look at our dialogue again and we'll be back.

104
