Are you serious?

133
00:06:44,610 --> 00:06:46,130
Nah, I'm just janking your chain.

134
00:06:46,550 --> 00:06:47,130
He'll be here soon.

135
00:06:47,310 --> 00:06:48,130
I saw him last week.

136
00:06:48,310 --> 00:06:49,230
He told me he would show up.

137
00:06:53,040 --> 00:06:56,920
Alright let's move on now to four key phrases on Fluency Builder.

138
00:06:58,430 --> 00:06:59,470
Fluency Builder.

139
00:07:00,330 --> 00:07:04,370
So the focus of today's Fluency Builder is small talk.

140
00:07:04,670 --> 00:07:10,490
These are phrases you can use when you're at a reunion or a dinner, especially with people you don't know very well.

141
00:07:10,850 --> 00:07:13,330
So let's jump in and look at this first phrase.

142
00:07:13,920 --> 00:07:15,320
Great to see you.

143
00:07:15,320 --> 00:07:15,490
That's right.

144
00:07:16,320 --> 00:07:18,870
So he says, hey Bill, wow, great to see you.

145
00:07:19,380 --> 00:07:24,030
So this is a nice little opening phrase saying, wow, I'm really excited to see you.

146
00:07:24,130 --> 00:07:25,270
I'm really happy to see you.

147
00:07:25,550 --> 00:07:25,770
Exactly.

148
00:07:26,520 --> 00:07:27,520
It means I'm happy.

149
00:07:27,520 --> 00:07:27,730
Look at you.

150
00:07:28,520 --> 00:07:29,110
Great to see you.

151
00:07:29,540 --> 00:07:31,940
You say this at the beginning of a conversation.

152
00:07:32,320 --> 00:07:32,590
That's right.

153
00:07:33,190 --> 00:07:35,890
And actually Bill responded by saying likewise.

154
00:07:36,570 --> 00:07:37,470
So we talked about this.

155
00:07:37,590 --> 00:07:39,390
He's saying, wow, great to see you too.

156
00:07:39,870 --> 00:07:43,570
Then he continues by saying, it's been a long time.

157
00:07:43,880 --> 00:07:48,220
This means that these two people, they have not seen each other in a very long time.

158
00:07:48,620 --> 00:07:54,160
You can also say, it's been so long or it's been a long time.

159
00:07:54,400 --> 00:07:58,300
That means it's been a long time since we last saw each other.

160
00:07:58,380 --> 00:07:58,820
That's right.

161
00:07:59,300 --> 00:08:01,200
So Robert goes on to ask Bill.

162
00:08:01,420 --> 00:08:03,400
He says, well, you know, how have you been?

163
00:08:03,940 --> 00:08:04,680
How are things?

164
00:08:05,760 --> 00:08:08,280
Bill answers by saying, can't complain.

165
00:08:09,040 --> 00:08:09,600
Okay.

166
00:08:09,600 --> 00:08:12,820
So this is not grammatically correct.

167
00:08:13,560 --> 00:08:14,740
Normally you need a subject.

168
00:08:15,480 --> 00:08:16,420
I can't complain.

169
00:08:17,240 --> 00:08:24,600
But when we're speaking in English and we're having a conversation like this, it's okay to say just can't complain as a phrase.

170
00:08:24,600 --> 00:08:26,020
So he's saying, how have you been?

171
00:08:26,640 --> 00:08:27,820
Well, I can't complain.

172
00:08:29,200 --> 00:08:29,480
Can't complain.

173
00:08:30,200 --> 00:08:31,700
That means nothing bad has happened.

174
00:08:32,120 --> 00:08:32,860
Things are okay.

175
00:08:33,440 --> 00:08:33,820
Right.

176
00:08:34,120 --> 00:08:35,060
You're saying that things are good.

177
00:08:35,240 --> 00:08:38,400
They're not fantastic, but they're not bad either.

178
00:08:38,400 --> 00:08:39,440
So you can't really complain.

179
00:08:40,320 --> 00:08:45,080
So it's just a phrase where very directly you can say, oh, can't complain.

180
00:08:45,420 --> 00:08:45,880
Everything's great.

181
00:08:46,330 --> 00:08:48,730
So you could even say this about other things.

182
00:08:49,160 --> 00:08:53,760
Like, you know, I asked John how his job was and he said, can't complain.

183
00:08:53,830 --> 00:08:53,950
Right.

184
00:08:54,450 --> 00:08:55,650
It's not great, but it's not bad.

185
00:08:56,160 --> 00:08:57,160
Right.

186
00:08:57,160 --> 00:08:58,160
Exactly right.

187
00:08:58,160 --> 00:08:59,160
All right.

188
00:08:59,160 --> 00:09:08,010
And obviously we saw how Robert lied to Bill by saying that their good friend, that their good friend Frank had passed away.

189
00:09:09,320 --> 00:09:10,650
And he said, are you serious?

190
00:09:11,320 --> 00:09:14,000
No, I'm just yanking your chain.

191
00:09:14,200 --> 00:09:23,720
Okay, so this might sound very strange to a lot of you because a chain is something that doesn't really seem to fit in this conversation.

192
00:09:25,000 --> 00:09:28,760
But yanking your chain, remember this as a phrase, it means I'm just joking.

193
00:09:28,760 --> 00:09:29,760
Right.

194
00:09:29,760 --> 00:09:30,760
I'm not serious.

195
00:09:30,760 --> 00:09:31,760
I'm just kidding.

196
00:09:31,760 --> 00:09:32,860
I'm lying to you, I guess.

197
00:09:33,800 --> 00:09:34,800
Right.

198
00:09:34,800 --> 00:09:36,520
So, but how can you use this in another context?

199
00:09:37,100 --> 00:09:37,320
Okay.

200
00:09:37,340 --> 00:09:39,580
I could say, you know what, Marco, I have something I have to tell you.

201
00:09:39,940 --> 00:09:40,420
And I say what?

202
00:09:41,840 --> 00:09:42,840
I'm leaving EnglishPod.

203
00:09:43,210 --> 00:09:43,430
I quit.

204
00:09:43,840 --> 00:09:44,840
I'm sorry.

205
00:09:44,840 --> 00:09:45,840
Are you serious?

206
00:09:45,840 --> 00:09:47,360
No, I'm just yanking your chain.

207
00:09:47,370 --> 00:09:47,670
Okay.

208
00:09:47,990 --> 00:09:49,570
Or no, I'm just kidding.

209
00:09:49,920 --> 00:09:50,920
Right.

210
00:09:50,920 --> 00:09:53,230
So you're kind of teasing someone when you do this, right?

211
00:09:53,840 --> 00:09:54,710
I'm just yanking your chain.

212
00:09:55,120 --> 00:09:56,130
I'm just lying to you.

213
00:09:56,210 --> 00:09:56,970
I'm teasing you.

214
00:09:57,190 --> 00:10:00,210
I'm making you believe something that isn't true.

215
00:10:00,720 --> 00:10:01,720
Exactly.

216
00:10:01,720 --> 00:10:07,000
So this is something you can use after you tell someone that you were just joking.

217
00:10:07,000 --> 00:10:08,790
So I'm just yanking your chain.

218
00:10:09,030 --> 00:10:10,250
I'm just joking with you.

219
00:10:10,560 --> 00:10:10,880
That's right.

220
00:10:11,760 --> 00:10:15,680
You can't translate it directly or literally because it's not going to make sense.

221
00:10:15,680 --> 00:10:19,440
So just remember this as a phrasal verb or a phrase in general.

222
00:10:19,440 --> 00:10:19,620
Exactly.

223
00:10:20,440 --> 00:10:21,440
All right.

224
